SpinDynamica
============

The `spyndynamica` package (src/spyndynamica), its tests and its reference
data were written by reading SpinDynamica 3.10.2
(https://www.spindynamica.soton.ac.uk), following its conventions and
comparing with results computed by it. No SpinDynamica source code is
included. Two data files of the package are taken from SpinDynamica: the
isotope table and physical constants (src/spyndynamica/data/isotopes.json)
and the tabulated orientational sampling sets
(src/spyndynamica/data/orientations.npz). The reference data of the tests
(tests/spyndynamica/fixtures/data) are results computed by SpinDynamica.

SpinDynamica is, in the words of its site
(https://www.spindynamica.soton.ac.uk/about/programmers/), programmed
mainly by Malcolm Levitt, with additional code and assistance by Jyrki
Rantaharju (speed enhancements), Andreas Brinkmann (parallelization and
powder averaging) and Soumya Singha Roy (isotope data). Christian Bengs
contributed code to its release 3.0 and is co-author of its publication;
the site names him as the author of AdiabaticTools and of the S(n)/SO(3)
dual pairing, subjects that release 3.10.2 covers and that are ported.

SpinDynamica names no formal licence and carries no copyright notice to
reproduce. Its terms of use
(https://www.spindynamica.soton.ac.uk/about/termsofuse/) state that
"SpinDynamica is open-source and freely available for use", and ask that
published work cite

    C. Bengs, M. H. Levitt, "SpinDynamica: Symbolic and numerical magnetic
    resonance in a Mathematica environment", Magn. Reson. Chem. (2017),
    doi:10.1002/mrc.4642,

and that the authors of any add-on used be credited as well. Work that uses
`spyndynamica` should cite that publication. The MIT permission above is
given by the author of the port for the port; it does not replace
SpinDynamica's own terms for what is SpinDynamica's. Keep this notice with
any copy or substantial portion of the `spyndynamica` package.


Use of AI in the translation
============================

The translation of Spinach and EasySpin from MATLAB and the port of
SpinDynamica from Mathematica to Python were carried out largely by
an AI coding agent (Claude, Anthropic) working under the direction of
Alexej Jerschow, who set the goals, reviewed the results and holds the
copyright above. Commits made by the agent are marked as such in the git
history. Correctness rests on the test suites, which compare each port with
reference data computed by its original, not on the method of authorship.
